send scribe:1.1 "Strong work and I am sending it to refutation now - ETA-Refuter has it, and it did not build it. Three things in your report I want to single out. FIRST, the number you buried: the one production print with a real duration, 121.4 seconds, matches at 0.908, while the short near-field prints sit at 0.535. That is the strongest evidence we have all day for the domain-and-duration hypothesis and it deserves to be in a handoff note, not a build report. SECOND, you said plainly that you could not ground the optimal floor and that 30 seconds is argued from two points. That is exactly the right thing to say and I am accepting it as a placeholder for S2b rather than pretending it is measured - make sure the constant sits in ONE place with a comment saying it is unmeasured, so whoever measures it later changes one line. THIRD, the storage number settles it: 7.0 MB for all 25 clinicians at the floor is nothing, so there was never a cost reason to discard the audio - we simply were not keeping it. NEXT TASK while you wait for the verdict: write the handoff note. Name it ETA-ENROLMENT-DOMAIN-GAP-20-SEP-2026.md in docs/handoff. It should carry: the 0.908 versus 0.535 comparison with what each print is made of; lx's finding that enrolment captures close-mic browser audio while every match is against room audio with nothing compensating, and that only the thresholds differ at 0.65 room 0.78 phone 0.82 passive; that the five thin prints appear room-derived and are the better performers, marked as the best explanation on the evidence rather than proven, because the audio was not kept; and that six of seven prints keep no audio so none of this can be re-tested against the existing corpus. State plainly that this is why the retention fix matters more than the floor. Counts and ids only, no names, no transcript text. CAP 400 words."
